the Trump administration's plan to roll  back mandatory insurance coverage for  birth control faces a new federal court  challenge Washington State's Attorney  General sued yesterday to stop the new  rules from taking effect California and  Massachusetts have already filed similar  complaints last week the administration  said it is changing the Obamacare  requirement that health plans cover  contraception it wants to exempt any  non-government employer with a quote  religious or moral objection Adriana  Diaz is on the campus of Notre Dame  University in Indiana which welcomes the  changes  Adriana good morning good morning  because of the Catholic Church has  strict stance against contraception  Notre Dame has fought the birth control  mandate for years and though it hasn't  decided to completely withdraw coverage  yet some women on this campus are  worried about losing a part of their  health care coverage I respect my  University's adherence to Catholicism  but I also think that no woman should  have to choose between her serious  medical needs and her University's  religion Notre Dame law student Kate  Roche signed up for the university  health plan just last year and it could  already be in for a drastic change the  school may drop birth control coverage  following the trumpet administration's  announcement Friday at this point in my  life I don't have a disposable income to  go out and seek out contraceptives or  like a private insurance provider the  new rules would give quote conscience  protections to employers based on their  religious or moral convictions this  includes nonprofits and publicly traded  companies under the Obama administration  only houses of worship could be exempt  come on up here sister come on up  president Trump signaled to the  regulatory changes in May when he  invited these nuns who had challenged  the birth control mandate in court to  the Rose Garden with this executive  order we are ending the attacks on your  religious liberty Houston Baptist  University President Robert Sloan says  the old rules forced his institution to  cover emergency contraception like plan  B which he considers a form of abortion  as the new rules protect freedom of  religion because the government does not  have the right to tell the Little  Sisters of the Poor  or Notre Dame or any Catholic  institution what its own religious  convictions are 58 percent of women use  oral contraceptive pills for reasons  beyond birth control like treating the  disease endometriosis or menstrual pain  those pills can cost more than a  thousand dollars a year without  insurance Roche a member of the ACLU is  now a plaintiff in the group's lawsuit  challenging the Trump administration  rules was it difficult for you to decide  to speak out coming forward about such a  personal matter is a really scary  prospect  but at the end of the day a lot of women  on campus feel that this is an unfair  intrusion into our private rights the  new regulations are expected to be  published on Friday the Trump  administration estimates that 200  employers could take advantage of the  exemption potentially impacting 120,000  women Norah  all right Adriana thank you
 

